# Coding Convention

## Descriptive Variable Names

Never use single-letter, two-letter, or generic variable names. Every variable name must clearly describe what the value represents in context.

### Forbidden patterns

- **Single/two-letter names:** `tx`, `db`, `cb`, `fn`, `el`, `ev`, `i`, `j`, `x`, `y`
- **Generic names:** `result`, `data`, `value`, `item`, `row`, `updated`, `response`, `output`, `temp`, `info`, `detail`, `obj`

### What to use instead

Variable names should describe **what the value is** in the specific context. A reader should understand what the variable holds without looking at the right-hand side of the assignment.

### Examples

Bad:
```typescript
const result = await compositeFoodService.update(id, payload);
const tx = await db.transaction();
const row = await ingredientRepository.findById(id);
const data = await fetch("/api/meals");
const items = orders.map((item) => item.total);
```

Good:
```typescript
const updatedCompositeFood = await compositeFoodService.update(compositeFoodId, payload);
const transaction = await database.transaction();
const ingredientRecord = await ingredientRepository.findById(ingredientId);
const mealResponse = await fetch("/api/meals");
const orderTotals = orders.map((order) => order.total);
```

### Loop variables and callbacks

Even in short callbacks, use descriptive names:

Bad:
```typescript
ingredients.map((i) => i.name);
meals.forEach((m) => m.prepare());
```

Good:
```typescript
ingredients.map((ingredient) => ingredient.name);
meals.forEach((meal) => meal.prepare());
```

## Debuggable Returns

Never `return` a function/method call directly. Always capture the result into a descriptively named variable on its own line, then `return` that variable. This lets a debugger stop on the `return` line with the value already inspectable.

Applies to all `return` statements: async (`return await fn(...)`), sync (`return fn(...)`), method chains (`return foo.bar().baz()`), built-in coercions (`return Number(x) > 0`), and `.map()`/`.filter()` callbacks.

### Examples

Bad:
```typescript
async findById(id: string): Promise<IngredientRow | null> {
  return await getDb().selectFrom("ingredients").selectAll().where("id", "=", id).executeTakeFirst();
}

async delete(id: string): Promise<boolean> {
  const result = await getDb().deleteFrom("ingredients").where("id", "=", id).executeTakeFirst();
  return Number(result.numDeletedRows) > 0;
}

items.map((item) => {
  return buildDetail(item);
});
```

Good:
```typescript
async findById(id: string): Promise<IngredientRow | null> {
  const ingredientRecord = await getDb().selectFrom("ingredients").selectAll().where("id", "=", id).executeTakeFirst();
  return ingredientRecord ?? null;
}

async delete(id: string): Promise<boolean> {
  const deleteResult = await getDb().deleteFrom("ingredients").where("id", "=", id).executeTakeFirst();
  const deletedCount = Number(deleteResult.numDeletedRows);
  return deletedCount > 0;
}

ingredients.map((ingredient) => {
  const ingredientDetail = buildDetail(ingredient);
  return ingredientDetail;
});
```

### Allowed Exceptions

- Early returns of literals / simple expressions: `return null;`, `return [];`, `return ingredientRecord ?? null;`, `return deletedCount > 0;` — these don't invoke a function, so a breakpoint on the line already shows everything.
- Arrow-function shorthand with no body (`(x) => x * 2`) — there's no `return` statement to break on.
